  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Michigan has $5.5B in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state recorded 155,913 awards with an average award size of $35.4K. Obligations fell 16.4% year over year. Annual obligations show a peak at $9B in FY22 and a steady decline to $5.5B in FY26. The Department of Defense is the largest buyer at $2.7B, followed by the General Services Administration at $1.8B. Automobile Manufacturing is the largest industry recipient with $1.8B, and Military Armored Vehicle, Tank, and Tank Component Manufacturing follows with $1.4B. GENERAL DYNAMICS LAND SYSTEMS INC. is the largest vendor at $1.3B. GENERAL MOTORS LLC, FORD MOTOR CO, and FCA US LLC are also major vendors with $633M, $619M, and $512M respectively. This mix of agencies, industries, and vendors highlights defense and automotive spending as the dominant components of Michigan federal contracting in the current fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "What changed in federal contracting year over year?",
        "body": "Obligations fell 16.4% year over year. Total obligations moved from $6.6B in FY25 to $5.5B in FY26. Award count and average award size are at 155,913 awards and $35.4K respectively in FY26 to date. The multi-year series shows a high of $9B in FY22 and a decline through FY26."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors drive Michigan contract dollars?",
        "body": "Automobile Manufacturing is the largest industry with $1.8B in obligations. Military Armored Vehicle, Tank, and Tank Component Manufacturing is second with $1.4B. GENERAL DYNAMICS LAND SYSTEMS INC. is the largest vendor at $1.3B, followed by GENERAL MOTORS LLC at $633M and FORD MOTOR CO at $619M. FCA US LLC contributes $512M, underscoring the concentration in automotive and defense suppliers."
      }
    ]
  },
